'''PilsenPDF''' (abbreviated '''P-PDF''') is a [[free and open-source software|free and open-source]] [[Portable Document Format|PDF]] viewer, annotator, and editor developed by Richard Lipka at the Faculty of Applied Sciences (FAV) of the [[University of West Bohemia]] in [[Plzeň]] (Pilsen) and released under the [[MIT License]].<ref name="github-repo" /><ref name="license" /> All document parsing, rendering, editing, and cryptographic signing run locally on the [[client-side]] in a [[web browser]] without uploading files to a remote server.<ref name="official-site">{{cite web |url=https://richardlipka.github.io/pilsen-pdf/ |title=PilsenPDF (P-PDF) – Client-Side Web PDF Editor |website=richardlipka.github.io |access-date=23 November 2026}}</ref> Alongside the browser edition hosted on [[GitHub Pages]], the project distributes a standalone [[Microsoft Windows|Windows]] desktop edition packaged from the same codebase using [[Electron (software framework)|Electron]].<ref name="github-releases" />

== Features ==

PilsenPDF organizes its interface into three workspaces—review and annotation, direct PDF editing, and signing—with localization in English and Czech and three switchable interface themes.<ref name="github-repo" /> Page-level operations include thumbnail reordering, rotation, batch deletion, inserting blank pages, and merging pages from other PDF files or raster images, while preserving unmodified pages and document outlines (<code>/Outlines</code>) on export in accordance with [[ISO 32000]]-1.<ref name="github-repo" />

Review and markup tools cover highlights, underlines, strikethroughs, sticky notes, freehand drawings, text boxes, and geometric shapes (<code>/Square</code>, <code>/Circle</code>, and <code>/Line</code>) exported with compiled PDF appearance streams (<code>/AP /N</code>).<ref name="github-repo" /> The editor also supports filling interactive [[AcroForm]] fields (with either interactive or flattened output), full-text in-document search, document metadata and [[Extensible Metadata Platform|XMP]] editing, raster flattening, and client-side visual and cryptographic [[PAdES]] / [[PKCS 7|PKCS#7]] detached digital signatures.<ref name="github-repo" />

Like low-level editors such as [[PDFedit]], PilsenPDF provides direct access to decompressed page content streams (<code>/Contents</code>) and Form XObjects.<ref name="github-repo" /> Users can inspect a page's semantic text and image tree, rewrite individual lines or multi-line <code>BT ... ET</code> text blocks while preserving surrounding layout and font encodings (substituting an embedded Unicode font only when required glyphs are missing), replace or extract embedded bitmap images, or edit content-stream operators directly.<ref name="github-repo" />

== Architecture ==

The web frontend is built as a single-page application in [[TypeScript]] and [[React (software)|React]], bundled with [[Vite (software)|Vite]] and styled with [[Tailwind CSS]] and Lucide vector icons.<ref name="react">{{cite web |url=https://react.dev/ |title=React – The library for web and native user interfaces |website=react.dev |access-date=23 November 2026}}</ref><ref name="typescript">{{cite web |url=https://www.typescriptlang.org/ |title=TypeScript: JavaScript With Syntax For Types |website=typescriptlang.org |access-date=23 November 2026}}</ref><ref name="vite">{{cite web |url=https://vite.dev/ |title=Vite – Next Generation Frontend Tooling |website=vite.dev |access-date=23 November 2026}}</ref><ref name="tailwind">{{cite web |url=https://tailwindcss.com/ |title=Tailwind CSS – Rapidly build modern websites |website=tailwindcss.com |access-date=23 November 2026}}</ref> Core PDF processing and signing rely on several client-side JavaScript libraries:

* '''[[PDF.js]]''' (<code>pdfjs-dist</code>), maintained by [[Mozilla]], renders pages onto an HTML5 [[Canvas element|Canvas]] inside a [[Web Worker]], extracts text layers and character coordinates, parses bookmarks, and supplies standard font and CMap assets.<ref name="pdfjs">{{cite web |url=https://mozilla.github.io/pdf.js/ |title=PDF.js – A general-purpose, web standards-based platform for parsing and rendering PDFs |publisher=[[Mozilla]] |access-date=23 November 2026}}</ref>
* '''pdf-lib''' together with '''fontkit''' handles low-level binary PDF structure modification, page-tree preservation, TrueType/OpenType Unicode font subsetting, AcroForm serialization, and ISO 32000-1 appearance stream export.<ref name="pdflib">{{cite web |url=https://pdf-lib.js.org/ |title=pdf-lib – Create and modify PDF documents in any JavaScript environment |website=pdf-lib.js.org |access-date=23 November 2026}}</ref><ref name="fontkit">{{cite web |url=https://github.com/foliojs/fontkit |title=foliojs/fontkit – An advanced font engine for Node and the browser |website=[[GitHub]] |access-date=23 November 2026}}</ref>
* '''node-forge''' performs in-browser [[PKCS 12|PKCS#12]] certificate decryption, [[RSA cryptosystem|RSA]] key generation, [[SHA-2|SHA-256]] byte-range hashing, and ASN.1 [[PKCS 7|PKCS#7]] detached signature construction.<ref name="nodeforge">{{cite web |url=https://github.com/digitalbazaar/forge |title=digitalbazaar/forge – A native implementation of TLS and other cryptographic tools in JavaScript |website=[[GitHub]] |access-date=23 November 2026}}</ref>
* '''signature_pad''' captures pressure-interpolated vector handwriting strokes for mouse, touch, and stylus input.<ref name="sigpad">{{cite web |url=https://github.com/szimek/signature_pad |title=szimek/signature_pad – HTML5 canvas based smooth signature drawing |website=[[GitHub]] |access-date=23 November 2026}}</ref>

The Windows desktop edition wraps the same self-contained web bundle inside an [[Electron (software framework)|Electron]] shell, adding native open and save dialogs, atomic in-place file saving, recent-file tracking, command-line file opening, and persistent local settings.<ref name="electron">{{cite web |url=https://www.electronjs.org/ |title=Electron – Build cross-platform desktop apps with JavaScript, HTML, and CSS |website=electronjs.org |access-date=23 November 2026}}</ref>

